    "video_id": "kenji:QRHBnO7z3OU",
    "purpose": "Show how Kenji reheats a bagel by dipping it whole in water and toasting it whole before slicing, and explain why this beats split-and-toast.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Toasting a bagel whole after a quick dip in water, and only then slicing it, brings back most of the fresh-baked contrast between a thin crisp shell and a chewy interior. Splitting and toasting makes every bagel taste much the same.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "A fresh bagel, under about two hours out of the oven, has an eggshell-thin crisp shell and a chewy, moist interior; that contrast fades quickly.",
      "Splitting and toasting acts as a 'bagel equalizer' that makes great and mediocre bagels taste similar.",
      "Dipping the whole bagel in water and toasting it whole for about six minutes re-crisps the outside and re-tenderizes the inside, getting about 90% of the way to fresh.",
      "The water keeps the crust from getting thick and hard before the middle heats through; Kenji learned the trick from Dan Pashman.",
      "Tiny micro-blisters on the crust add surface area and crunch and are a sign of a great bagel.",
      "Bagel shops will toast a bagel whole before slicing if you ask.",
      "Mix fresh scallions into cream cheese rather than buying it pre-mixed; it can be made a day or two ahead."
    ],
